Extreme drought and flood occurrences are strongly associated with the El Ni$\~{
i}$o--Southern Oscillation (ENSO) phenomenon. The complexity of ENSO is attributed to the presence of multiple phenomena that occur in the Equatorial Eastern Pacific. These include distinct modes of development: a gradual build-up of an ocean-atmosphere anomaly and rapid shifts tied to Kelvin wave activity. Patterns of low-pressure systems are frequently tied to convective instability triggered by anomalous eastward wind bursts, which in turn affect precipitation levels. To better understand the impact of different El Ni$\~{
i}o$ situations on global rainfall distribution, researchers analyzed two extensive datasets of land-based precipitation occurrences. These datasets were constructed by identifying years of extreme drought and flood, followed by associating them with corresponding sea-surface-temperature-spreads to pinpoint instances of Eastern Pacific anomalous warmth and coolness. Next, they performed a low-frequency (less than 2 years) spectral analysis of the resulting time series to determine how various El Ni$\~{
i}o$-associated patterns influence precipitation probabilities. The results revealed that regions affected by slow-growing ocean-atmosphere dynamics and those influenced by rapid-shifts associated with gravity-wave activity have significantly differential spatial associations with both low-pressure and high-pressure systems. The distinction between these regimes highlights the importance of considering both temperature spread and temporal precipitation dynamics to fully understanding El Ni$\~{
i}o$'s complex global impact.
